00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:38.080
it could have been done with the hand a weapon&nbsp;
or a whip but it was most commonly done with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:42.880
coup stick a long peeled wand with a feather on&nbsp;
the end the challenge was getting close enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:48.080
to the enemy to touch him and then escape without&nbsp;
getting injured or killed it was generally seen as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:53.120
more honorable than killing or scalping the enemy&nbsp;
but at the same time it was a great humiliation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:58.160
for the target so most native warriors would have&nbsp;
gone to great lengths to avoid being the ones that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:02.800
someone else counted kuan anyway when he&nbsp;
was 14 jumping badger was presented with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:06.640
a coup stick and joined a lakota war&nbsp;
party on a raid against the crow who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:10.800
were their greatest enemy at the time during the&nbsp;
fight the young man was successful in counting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:14.880
coup against a crow warrior this filled his&nbsp;
father with pride and when they returned home&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:19.600
the tribe had a ceremony to honor the young&nbsp;
warrior and mark his passage into adulthood he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:27.840
also received a new name that was more worthy&nbsp;
of him and jumping badger became sitting bull

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:35.440
at 14 sitting ball was already regarded as a&nbsp;
warrior and he continued his meteoric rise through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:40.160
the ranks of the lakota tribe he was a member of&nbsp;
several groups most notably of the strong heart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:44.720
society which was made up of the bravest and most&nbsp;
elite warriors he was then given the prestigious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:50.880
title of sash wearer and became the leader of the&nbsp;
group in 1856. sitting ball proved himself to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:56.560
a natural leader and it was only fitting that he&nbsp;
became one of the people who shaped their tribe's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.560 --> 00:05:01.440
relationships with the outside world particularly&nbsp;
after his father was killed in a fight with a crow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:06.480
warrior at first he was indifferent to the u.s&nbsp;
government and white settlers he said i have seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:12.640
nothing that the white man has which is as good as&nbsp;
our right to roam and live on the open plains as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:18.000
we choose that being said he wasn't hostile either&nbsp;
growing up most of the interactions with white&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:22.960
people that he experienced were positive since&nbsp;
most of them involved fur traders who were eager&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:28.320
to stay on friendly terms with the lakota but as&nbsp;
settlers and prospectors kept expanding westward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:34.720
animosity developed on both sides that inevitably&nbsp;
turned violent this resulted in the dakota war of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:41.200
1862 also known by many other names such as the&nbsp;
sioux uprising or little crows war the conflict&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:45.760
started in minnesota which had only become a&nbsp;
state a few years earlier to help colonize the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.760 --> 00:05:51.600
territory the u.s government passed the homestead&nbsp;
act in 1862 which promised 160 acres of land to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:57.040
any person or family who claimed that land and&nbsp;
then lived and cultivated it for five years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.040 --> 00:06:01.680
most of that territory originally belonged&nbsp;
to native american groups such as the dakota&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:06.640
and ojibwe people on paper the american government&nbsp;
obtained all the land fair and square by either&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:11.120
buying it directly or setting up reservations&nbsp;
where the native tribes were supposed to receive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:15.440
regular payments and supply shipments but the&nbsp;
government regularly broke the treaties which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:19.920
were heavily in their favor to begin with payments&nbsp;
were missed supplies were late and contracts were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:25.120
renegotiated to get more and more concessions&nbsp;
by 1858 the dakota people from minnesota lived&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:29.600
on a small strip of land and many of them were&nbsp;
starving due to the lack of food shipments and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:35.120
their inability to hunt frustration turned to&nbsp;
anger which turned to violence in 1862 a faction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:39.600
of dakota warriors led by chief little crow&nbsp;
began raiding settlements and stores launching a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.600 --> 00:06:45.360
six-week long war that saw the deaths of over 350&nbsp;
settlers the civil war slowed down the government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:50.560
response but the dakota eventually fought an&nbsp;
army consisting mainly of volunteers they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:55.120
decisively defeated at the battle of wood lake&nbsp;
and littlecrow was later killed during a raiding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.120 --> 00:06:59.440
party afterwards dozens of dakota warriors&nbsp;
were hanged for their role in the war and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.440 --> 00:07:04.160
the government voided all treaties with the dakota&nbsp;
people forcing them to leave minnesota altogether

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:14.880
sitting bull had no role in this war but his&nbsp;
people and many other tribes suffered the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:19.680
consequences all the same as the government&nbsp;
decided to punish all the sioux by sending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:24.080
military expeditions into their territory to&nbsp;
push them back even further it was in june&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.080 --> 00:07:29.760
1863 that sitting bull first fought the united&nbsp;
states army but this was just a minor skirmish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:36.480
a real fight occurred a year later on july 28 1864&nbsp;
at the battle of kildare mountain this conflict&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:41.600
involved around 4 000 soldiers in total which may&nbsp;
have been the largest single battle between the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.600 --> 00:07:47.520
u.s army and the plains tribes larger than the&nbsp;
more famous battle of little bighorn that we'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:52.960
cover in a bit on one side there were two brigades&nbsp;
led by brigadier general alfred sully around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:58.320
2200 soldiers their mission usually referred&nbsp;
to as sully's expedition was to weaken the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:03.440
sioux however possible and get them to relocate&nbsp;
further west best way to do this was to destroy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:09.120
their villages they had already done it once back&nbsp;
at white stone hill back in september 1863 and now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:15.040
they had their sights set on a new target an even&nbsp;
larger sioux encampment near the little missouri&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.040 --> 00:08:21.120
river in present-day dunn county north dakota on&nbsp;
the other side were around 1 600 warriors most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:26.960
of them belonging to various lakota tribes sitting&nbsp;
bull was there as the leader of the papa alongside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:32.320
another famed warrior named gaul sally was&nbsp;
expecting an enemy force double even triple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.320 --> 00:08:37.760
what he actually encountered but he had superior&nbsp;
firepower which also included two cannons the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.760 --> 00:08:42.080
fight was fairly one-sided that lakota tried a&nbsp;
few times to charge into the ranks of the army&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.080 --> 00:08:46.480
soldiers but their guns kept the warriors at&nbsp;
bay until they finally reached within cannon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.480 --> 00:08:51.120
range once that happened the battle was pretty&nbsp;
much over sully batted the village with cannon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:56.080
fire until the inhabitants had no choice but to&nbsp;
abandon it the following day his men marched into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.080 --> 00:09:00.640
the settlement and destroyed everything including&nbsp;
food and supplies so that the lakota would have no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:05.120
reason to return for many warriors like sitting&nbsp;
bull it was the first time that they encountered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.120 --> 00:09:10.000
artillery in battle and were finally realizing&nbsp;
the strength that they were up against even so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:14.720
they did not retreat completely and instead kept&nbsp;
harassing sully's troops with small raids as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.720 --> 00:09:19.520
the army made its way through the rugged hills&nbsp;
and the steep ravines west of kildare mountain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.520 --> 00:09:24.080
this eventually culminated in the battle of&nbsp;
the badlands almost two weeks after their first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:28.640
conflict once again superior firepower won the&nbsp;
day and sully ended his military campaign once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.640 --> 00:09:34.080
the lakota had been driven into montana during&nbsp;
this time sitting bull was hardly the only lakota&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:38.960
leader who fought the united states army in&nbsp;
1866 another violent conflict emerged known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.960 --> 00:09:43.840
as red cloud's war which was led by chief&nbsp;
red clown leader of the oglala lakota people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.840 --> 00:09:48.320
for two years he fought the u.s army for control&nbsp;
of the powder river country region in wyoming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.320 --> 00:09:52.160
strictly speaking when a treaty had been&nbsp;
signed that land belonged to the crow people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.160 --> 00:09:57.120
however the oglala fought them over it the same&nbsp;
way that they had done for centuries and they won&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.120 --> 00:10:01.840
so they considered the territory rightfully theirs&nbsp;
then the u.s government tried taking advantage of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.840 --> 00:10:06.400
the situation to drive them away since that was an&nbsp;
important piece of land that territory contained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.400 --> 00:10:10.960
part of the bozeman trail which at that time&nbsp;
was the shortest route that took settlers and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.960 --> 00:10:16.320
prospectors from the oregon trail in wyoming to&nbsp;
the gold rush region of montana it was a route&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.320 --> 00:10:20.720
that the government desperately wanted to control&nbsp;
so they tried building several new forts to gain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.720 --> 00:10:25.600
a foothold in the area what made this conflict&nbsp;
notable was that it ended in a victory for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.600 --> 00:10:30.800
lakota for most of the war red cloud and his&nbsp;
allies which included sitting bull resorted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.800 --> 00:10:36.480
raids and other blitz attacks that being said&nbsp;
there were a few large-scale battles such as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:41.200
the fetterman fight which ended in decisive&nbsp;
victories for the dakota eventually the u.s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.200 --> 00:10:46.960
government sought peace and signed the treaty&nbsp;
