00:10:59.680 --> 00:11:04.000
sacred to the lakota as their place of&nbsp;
origin and granted them exclusive use of it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:09.360
around 130 tribal leaders signed the treaty of&nbsp;
fort laramie but sitting bull was not one of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.360 --> 00:11:12.960
he didn't do it because he didn't want to move&nbsp;
his people or change their way of life but his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.960 --> 00:11:18.240
actions proved somewhat prophetic because it took&nbsp;
the united states government less than a decade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:28.160
to break the treaty once they discovered&nbsp;
that there was gold in those hills

00:11:28.160 --> 00:11:32.880
throughout the late 1860s and early 1870s&nbsp;
sitting bull continued to carry out minor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.880 --> 00:11:38.000
skirmishes against various parties that entered&nbsp;
lakota territory most notable of all being a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.000 --> 00:11:42.640
group working for the northern pacific railway who&nbsp;
wanted to lay down track over land which sitting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.640 --> 00:11:47.440
bull considered to belong to the hong papa some&nbsp;
sources claim that during this time sitting bull&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.440 --> 00:11:52.480
became the supreme chief of sioux nation but&nbsp;
others strongly contend that no such position&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.480 --> 00:11:57.840
existed at most sitting bull came to be regarded&nbsp;
as the unofficial leader of the non-treaty lakota&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.840 --> 00:12:03.520
meaning all the native americans who refused to&nbsp;
sign and adhere to the treaty of fort laramie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.520 --> 00:12:08.400
and lived outside the great sioux reservation&nbsp;
in 1874 the united states government dispatched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.400 --> 00:12:12.720
the seventh cavalry to the black hills inside&nbsp;
the dakota territory their mission was to scout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.720 --> 00:12:18.720
for a suitable location for a new fort but also to&nbsp;
search the area for natural resources specifically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.720 --> 00:12:23.040
they were to investigate a rumor that there might&nbsp;
be gold in the black hills this was a time when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.040 --> 00:12:28.400
america was firmly gripped with gold fever the&nbsp;
california gold rush had a massive effect on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.400 --> 00:12:33.440
country's economy and its migration toward the&nbsp;
west so the possibility of a similar scenario&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.440 --> 00:12:40.400
in dakota was just too good to ignore therefore&nbsp;
on july the 2nd 1874 a thousand soldiers and 110&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.400 --> 00:12:46.880
wagons set off on the black hills expedition led&nbsp;
by lieutenant colonel george armstrong custer the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.880 --> 00:12:52.320
california gold rush also had a devastating effect&nbsp;
on the native american population and in august&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.320 --> 00:12:58.240
1874 the worst thing possible for them happened&nbsp;
custer found gold in the black hills he described&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.240 --> 00:13:03.200
as an extensive area rich in gold deposit and&nbsp;
in just a week the new york tribune proclaimed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.200 --> 00:13:08.480
the black hills to be new gold country nowhere in&nbsp;
custer's report or the newspapers was it mentioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.480 --> 00:13:14.640
that the land was the gold was legally the lakotas&nbsp;
by autumn over 15 000 miners have already made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.640 --> 00:13:18.640
their way to the dakota territory at first&nbsp;
the army was there to arrest and evacuate the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.640 --> 00:13:23.840
prospectors who snuck into lakota land although&nbsp;
it was mainly saving them from being killed by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.840 --> 00:13:28.560
native americans the government made an offer of&nbsp;
six million dollars to purchase the black hills an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.560 --> 00:13:33.440
offer which was firmly rejected by sitting bull at&nbsp;
that point president ulysses s grant pretty much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.440 --> 00:13:38.240
said screwed the treaty and pulled the army from&nbsp;
the region allowing people to travel unimpeded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.240 --> 00:13:44.800
to the black hills in december 1875 grant gave&nbsp;
a deadline of january 31 1876 by which point all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.800 --> 00:13:49.600
the sioux people had to relocate inside the great&nbsp;
sioux reservation those who failed to do so would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.600 --> 00:13:54.240
be considered and treated as hostiles even if&nbsp;
sitting bull would have been willing to move which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.240 --> 00:13:58.880
he wasn't it would have been almost impossible&nbsp;
to do it in such a short time and during the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.880 --> 00:14:04.080
middle of winter therefore he saw no other choice&nbsp;
the deadline passed and the great sioux war began

00:14:09.360 --> 00:14:13.600
word of sitting bull's refusal to accept any kind&nbsp;
of deal with the american government reached other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.600 --> 00:14:18.720
tribes that were living outside the great zoo&nbsp;
reservation the first half of 1876 or many of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.720 --> 00:14:23.600
them make their way to sitting bull's camp which&nbsp;
soon turned into a village with around 10 000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.600 --> 00:14:28.400
people on the bank of the little bighorn river&nbsp;
these were mainly lakota and dakota sioux but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.400 --> 00:14:34.240
also some northern cheyenne and arapaho the&nbsp;
exact number of warriors remains disputed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.240 --> 00:14:38.240
this day but it's generally estimated to&nbsp;
be around 2 000 and besides sitting bull&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.240 --> 00:14:42.960
other prominent leaders included garl and the&nbsp;
famed warrior crazy horse the opening skirmish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.960 --> 00:14:48.160
of the conflict which was also known as the black&nbsp;
hills war occurred in may 1876 when six cavalry&nbsp;&nbsp;

